module.exports = "# About Adya Srivastava\nI research how language models solve problems, and build the ML systems around them.\nBITS Pilani graduate (Electrical and Electronics, minor in Finance, 2026). I like questions where the benchmark number is not the whole story: whether a model computed or recalled, whether its confidence means anything, whether a result survives a change that should not matter. I'm looking for a team that works on hard versions of those questions.\nStatus: Open to ML research and research engineering roles\nEmail: srivastavadya@gmail.com\nGitHub: https://github.com/Adya6714\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/in/adyasri\n\n# Papers\n- Same Score, Different Strategy [CAISc 2026]: Multi-probe diagnosis of structural fragility in LLM reasoning. https://openreview.net/forum?id=d8w4gMVQ1w https://adya6714.github.io/retrieval-vs-computation/ https://github.com/Adya6714/retrieval-vs-computation\n- Reading Without Looking [Preprint]: Does OCR confidence track visual evidence? A controlled study on Indic scripts. https://adya6714.github.io/vlm-ocr-eval/ https://adya6714.github.io/vlm-ocr-eval/paper/main.pdf https://github.com/Adya6714/vlm-ocr-eval\n- Diffusion-Generated Volatility Surfaces for RL Hedging of NIFTY 50 Barrier Options [Draft]: A model-risk decomposition of RL hedging trained on generated market data. https://drive.google.com/file/d/1eXAm7XeUYIWnzu_3MWRpSY6nO9gyGDNV/view?usp=drive_link https://github.com/Adya6714/ddpm-vol-hedging\n- Hybrid Quantum-Classical Paradigms for Nonlinear Dynamics, ML and Autonomous Systems [Survey]: Carleman linearization, variational algorithms for PDEs, quantum RL, hardware comparisons. https://drive.google.com/file/d/1zwhnW2Nvl6I5myx556UoGED-1MKv9qt5/view?usp=sharing\n- Retrieval vs Computation, papers 2 to 4 [Planned]: Where the split lives inside the network, which training conditions produce it, and how it changes across scale. \n\n# Research notes (results)\n## A rename erased a perfect score (Retrieval vs Computation)\nQuestion: Does a model that solves a problem perfectly still solve it when only the names change?\nResult: On weighted interval scheduling, o3-mini scored a perfect 1.00 on the original problems and 0.00 after the entities were renamed. Every model that solved this subtype at all collapsed under the rename. Exposure and difficulty move together on this subtype, so this result alone cannot say which one is responsible.\nWhy it matters: A benchmark would have reported this model as fully capable on the task. The rename shows the capability did not survive a change that leaves the problem identical.\n\n## One rename, very different effects (Retrieval vs Computation)\nQuestion: If models simply recall memorised problems, shouldn't a rename hurt them all in the same way?\nResult: The same rename helped three models substantially, left two at the floor, and even reversed direction across problem subtypes. On arithmetic, Gemini stayed steady when the numbers were regenerated (0.909 to 0.958) but fell to 0.523 when only the names changed.\nWhy it matters: That spread is hard to explain with one shared retrieval mechanism. It points to model-specific strategies hiding under similar scores.\n\n## Accepting a wrong step is not derailing (Retrieval vs Computation)\nQuestion: If a model accepts a wrong intermediate value, does its answer go wrong too?\nResult: When I injected an incorrect state mid-solve, compliant models accepted it 88 to 100% of the time. Accuracy after injection stayed close to uninterrupted performance (Claude 52.5%, GPT-4o 50.8%, o3-mini 37.7%), and whether a model complied did not predict whether it finished correctly.\nWhy it matters: Following an injected value is not evidence that a model reasons from its own steps. Compliance and derailment have to be measured separately.\n\n## Most instances stay ambiguous (Retrieval vs Computation)\nQuestion: Can three behavioural probes label each problem as retrieved or computed?\nResult: Combining the probes into a per-instance label sounds like the goal. Under strict thresholds, 61.6% of instances stayed ambiguous, so the labels work as calibration points rather than a verdict.\nWhy it matters: It sets an honest bar for the next papers: per-instance diagnosis needs instruments validated beyond behaviour, which is where the mechanism work starts.\n\n## My harness caught my own bugs (Retrieval vs Computation)\nQuestion: How do you know a probe measures the model and not your own verifier?\nResult: Every probe was checked gold in, gold out: feed the verifier known-correct answers and confirm they score as correct. Those checks caught several verifier bugs in my own pipeline before they reached the results.\nWhy it matters: Behavioural probes are fragile instruments. Checking the instrument is part of the experiment, not cleanup.\n\n## 90% confident, almost never right (Reading Without Looking)\nQuestion: When an OCR model says it is 90% sure, is that about the image?\nResult: At the first character, where no earlier text can help, my OCR model reported about 0.90 confidence while the correct character had roughly 2×10⁻¹¹ probability. It was the top choice 0 times in 180 evaluations.\nWhy it matters: Confidence measured how peaked the output was, not whether the image supported the answer.\n\n## A blank page gets the same confidence (Reading Without Looking)\nQuestion: If confidence depends on vision, removing the image should lower it. Does it?\nResult: Blank, noise and scrambled images barely moved confidence (0.994 for text against 0.990 for a blank page). Switching off the vision pathway changed about 8% of predictions while confidence stayed almost flat.\nWhy it matters: A routing system that trusts confidence would wave exactly these pages through.\n\n## Good AUROC can still mislead (Reading Without Looking)\nQuestion: Is confidence useful for separating right from wrong answers?\nResult: On familiar lines, confidence separated right from wrong reasonably well (AUROC 0.838). On held-out lines it dropped to about 0.57, with a calibration error of 0.811.\nWhy it matters: A meter that looks good in-sample can be close to useless for decisions on new pages. Held-out checks have to come before any routing claim.\n\n# Projects\n## Retrieval vs Computation\nA measurement toolkit for telling whether an LLM reasoned or recalled.\nProblem: Benchmarks say whether an answer is right, not how the model got there. Two models with the same score can fail in very different ways once deployed.\nBuilt: Three probes: rewording and renaming, plan-versus-execution consistency, and closeness to training data. 219 problems across arithmetic, planning and optimisation, run on five models (about 20,000 API calls), with exact checkers for every answer. A reproducible pipeline: raw runs, derived metrics, paper tables and tests all regenerate from code.\nImpact: Gives teams a way to judge which models to trust on unseen problems, not just which score highest. Phase one of a four-paper program on where retrieval and computation split inside LLMs.\nLinks: https://adya6714.github.io/retrieval-vs-computation/ https://github.com/Adya6714/retrieval-vs-computation https://openreview.net/forum?id=d8w4gMVQ1w\n\n## Reading Without Looking\nCan you trust OCR confidence on Indic scripts?\nProblem: Document systems auto-accept pages with high confidence and send the rest to people. That only works if confidence tracks what the model actually sees, and Indic scripts make this hard to measure.\nBuilt: Grapheme-level scoring and a HarfBuzz renderer that produces real Indic script shaping. A 19.6M-parameter OCR model trained from scratch, so every internal step can be inspected. Counterfactual probes an API cannot run (blank, noise and scrambled images, removing the vision pathway), plus a transfer test on a production OCR API with pre-registered statistics.\nImpact: Shows peak confidence can stay high while the image contributes almost nothing, so routing on it alone can fail. Proposes grounding-aware confidence instead.\nLinks: https://adya6714.github.io/vlm-ocr-eval/ https://github.com/Adya6714/vlm-ocr-eval https://adya6714.github.io/vlm-ocr-eval/paper/main.pdf\n\n## Synthetic Volatility Surfaces for Hedging\nDiffusion-generated NIFTY 50 volatility surfaces to train an RL hedging agent.\nProblem: Training a hedging agent needs far more market scenarios than history provides, and an agent trained on one model of the market inherits that model's blind spots.\nBuilt: A dataset of 1,268 NIFTY implied volatility surfaces (2018 to 2026) built from 912K+ NSE option rows. A conditional diffusion model that generates new surfaces with fewer arbitrage violations than market data (penalty 0.005 vs 0.009). A PPO hedging agent for barrier options, compared against a Heston-trained RL baseline.\nImpact: Cut tail-risk hedging error (CVaR 95%) by 60.1% against Black-Scholes, and separates how much of the result comes from the generator versus the agent.\nLinks: https://github.com/Adya6714/ddpm-vol-hedging https://drive.google.com/file/d/1eXAm7XeUYIWnzu_3MWRpSY6nO9gyGDNV/view?usp=drive_link\n\n## OmniMesh\nAn offline disaster-response network with an AI triage backend.\nProblem: In disasters, internet and cell networks fail first, exactly when responders need to coordinate and triage.\nBuilt: Android phones act as triage nodes in an offline-first, peer-to-peer mesh. A six-agent FastAPI backend serving Gemma-2-9B with vLLM on an AMD GPU. Sync to the cloud once connectivity returns.\nImpact: Keeps communication and decision support working when infrastructure is down. Built for the AMD Developer Hackathon ACT II and entered in the Google Solution Challenge 2025.\nLinks: https://github.com/Adya6714/OmniMesh\n\n## CloudWatch in Plain English\nAsk AWS metrics questions in natural language and get charts back.\nProblem: Finding the right CloudWatch metric means knowing its exact name and digging through the console or CLI.\nBuilt: A Bedrock agent describes every metric; descriptions become embeddings stored in S3 with metadata in DynamoDB, refreshed weekly. Queries are embedded and matched by cosine similarity inside Lambda, then live data is pulled and charted. Ships as an npm package with a cross-account CDK construct that provisions all permissions.\nImpact: Any team can import it and query its metrics in plain language, without paying for a managed vector database.\nLinks: none\n\n## FraudScope360\nMultimodal fraud detection. National winner, Citi Bank Campus Innovation Challenge.\nProblem: Fraud rarely looks suspicious one account at a time. It shows up in the connections between accounts, identities and behaviour.\nBuilt: A 5-module engine combining graph embeddings, anomaly detection and XGBoost on engineered features. Coverage for fraud rings and identity fraud, served through FastAPI.\nImpact: Combines several weak signals into one decision, closer to how real fraud teams work than a single classifier.\nLinks: none\n\n## FraudSense AI\nDetecting LLM-assisted cheating in interviews.\nProblem: Candidates can read answers generated by an LLM during a live interview.\nBuilt: Fuses DetectGPT-style text signals, biometric cues and an LLM judge into one score.\nImpact: Gives interviewers a combined signal instead of a single brittle detector.\nLinks: none\n\n## Data in Motion\nDeciding where cloud data should live, then moving it.\nProblem: Storage tiers and regions trade off cost against latency, and the right placement changes as access patterns change.\nBuilt: A mixed-integer program chooses placement; a Kafka pipeline carries out migrations.\nImpact: Turns storage placement into an optimisation problem rather than a manual decision.\nLinks: none\n\n# Experience\n## EquiContracts: Founding Engineer, 2026 to present\n- Building an agentic platform for construction contract operations in India: bank guarantee tracking, milestone and payment validation, and an evidence locker shared by contractor and client.\n- Stack: FastAPI, Next.js, Postgres with row-level security, a YAML-driven deterministic rules engine and an isolated AI extraction package.\n\n## Nurix AI: ML Engineering Intern, Jul to Dec 2025\n- Built and deployed a TinyBERT + NN voicemail detector with backend integration (95% precision).\n- Improved real-time turn-end detection in a transformer speech-to-text pipeline using semantic parsing.\n- Designed a word-error correction pipeline (Word2Vec, phonetic normalisation, LLM scoring) that cut WER by 40%.\n\n## FidelFolio Investments: Quant Investment Analyst, May to Jun 2025\n- Built a research workflow for portfolio analytics and Sharpe evaluation, improving research efficiency by 90%.\n- Used genetic algorithms and Bayesian optimisation for risk-aware portfolios, improving Sharpe by about 42%.\n- Analysed 24 factors across 200+ portfolios.\n\n## Grasim (Aditya Birla Group): Research Engineer, May to Jul 2024\n- Designed 20+ PLC-based railway simulations for autonomous control and safety validation.\n- Engineered IR and IoT sensor networks for track monitoring with 98% trespassing detection accuracy.\n\n## Khageshvara Aviation: Product Development Lead and ML Intern, May to Oct 2023\n- Developed CNN aerial vision pipelines for real-time human detection on UAVs.\n- Built financial models and pitch strategy that helped secure DST funding.\n\n# Skills\n- LLMs and NLP: PyTorch (used in All research projects), Transformers (used in RvC, Nurix speech pipeline), LLM evaluation (used in Retrieval vs Computation), Probing and ablation (used in Reading Without Looking), LangChain (used in Agent tooling)\n- Vision: OpenCV (used in UAV vision), OCR (used in Indic OCR research), CNNs (used in Aerial detection)\n- RL and generative: Diffusion models (used in Volatility surfaces), PPO (used in Hedging agent), vLLM (used in OmniMesh)\n- Systems: Python (used in Everything), FastAPI (used in OmniMesh, FraudScope360, EquiContracts), Docker (used in Deployments), AWS (used in CloudWatch agent), GCP (used in Cloud work), SQL (used in Data work), TypeScript (used in Web builds)\n- Quant and classical ML: XGBoost (used in FraudScope360), Graph ML (used in FraudScope360), Portfolio optimisation (used in FidelFolio), Volatility modelling (used in NIFTY surfaces), CVaR (used in Hedging evaluation)\n\n# Education\nB.E. Electrical and Electronics Engineering, BITS Pilani, 2026, minor in Finance.";
